e gig  economy who are earning between six  hundred dollars and twenty thousand  dollars uh though it's worth mentioning  that this can have some benefit for  those folks in that they may also  receive this form from the irs so that  will give them some more transparency on  what they could potentially be owing or  what's being reported to the irs as a  transaction  also it gives them a fresh way to look  at perhaps where they will might be able  to take deductions on some of their  business expenses and reduce their  taxable income the irs is not in great  shape i mean they don't they don't have  enough funding they don't have enough  resources how likely is it that small  business owners would be audited if they  decided to ignore this tax form that  gets sent from venmo so the likelihood  of being audited overall especially if  you're not a very high earner is remains  pretty low and it still remains low even  after this reporting change because the  irs has seen their audit rates go down  over the last 10 years  and so if you are in a situation where  you're receiving this form say for  personal reasons and you're worried  about being audited on that uh it's  probably not a high risk not something  to worry about if you are worried and  you want to mitigate any potential risk  having information on hand on the nature  of those transactions is always really  helpful a really good example of that is  for example if you're taking a rent  payment on behalf of a roommate and  relaying it to your landlord that could  add up to you know north of ten thousand  dollars a year uh but that's pretty  straightforward situation where you can  keep those records uh if you're a small  business owner of course i think it just  underscores the importance of just just  maintaining the best practices of your  business right separating out your  business and personal accounts keeping  receipts if you're making deductions  those are things you should already be  doing but this reporting change just  makes that more important because it  might highlight some of these potential  issue areas if you're not doing that to  the irs okay so keep good records is  your advice here so i have to admit  garrett when i first saw this news my  first thought was drug dealers are all  going to move back to cash or crypto i  mean they're going to run away from  these different platforms  yeah i think there is going to be as a  trade-off to this some incentive for  folks who are not  above board on their transactions to  move away from these platforms and that  is going to be  one thing that the irs and treasury will  have to have to monitor if there are  other  ways to try to get at those types of  transactions there's already of course  monitoring in the financial system for  illicit activity and there might be a  bit more of that as a trade-off of  trying to be a bit more transparent with  these digital platforms uh and so that  will have to be part of and that really  shows that this is a holistic discussion  right you can try to plug one hole in uh  reporting in the tax system but that  will create behaviors that show up  elsewhere uh in crypto and elsewhere in  the financial system so that is an  important consideration as policymakers  think of next steps on reporting yeah  absolutely everyone in fintech likes to  talk about how the future is cashless  cash will soon become obsolete we're all  going to be like sweden but its policy  changes like the one that we're dealing  with right now that will keep cash  really more popular than ever the  thinking is that cash transactions are  non-taxable and are and therefore or i  should say that they're not traceable  and therefore not taxable how would you  respond to that  right so in the tax system generally  speaking the nature of how you pay a  transaction or  try to work with folks financially it  has no bearing on whether or not a  transaction is taxable so if you're  taking a cash for a particular uh for  revenue somewhere that income may still  be just as taxable as if you took it  digitally and while it's true that cash  can have some looking some challenges  for folks to trace that if it does have  any interaction in the financial system  if you put that cash into a into a  checking account or withdraw it  elsewhere there will be records that tie  that cash to you so unless you're you're  in an operation that's fairly  sophisticated there's still going to be  a lot of risk in in doing it that way uh  best practice is to remain above board  and report the cash stuff that you are  getting via tips or other forms of  revenue  garrett great stuff thank you so much  really appreciate it garrett watson  joining us from the tax foundation a lot  to think about come tax filing season of  course it does not apply for 2021 taxes  it only applies to 2022 taxes so this is  something we'll be dealing with in april  of 2023  this is bloomer quick take i'm scarlet  faux
 

